  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $f$ and $g$ are both strictly increasing functions on $\\mathbb R$. Give a proof or counterexample for each of the following statements:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p33-q8a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f+g$ is strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.\n\nFor $a<b$, add $f(a)<f(b)$ and $g(a)<g(b)$.",
          "worked": "Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ with $a<b$. Since both functions are strictly increasing, $f(a)<f(b)$ and $g(a)<g(b)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(f+g)(a)&=f(a)+g(a)\\\\(f+g)(a)&<f(b)+g(b)\\\\(f+g)(a)&<(f+g)(b).\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence $f+g$ is strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q8b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f\\cdot g$ is strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. Take $f(x)=g(x)=x$, so $(f\\cdot g)(x)=x^2$.\n\nAlthough $-1<0$, $(f\\cdot g)(-1)=1>0=(f\\cdot g)(0)$.",
          "worked": "Take $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=x$. Both are strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$, but\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(f\\cdot g)(x)&=x^2\\\\(f\\cdot g)(-1)&=1\\\\(f\\cdot g)(0)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $-1<0$ but $(f\\cdot g)(-1)>(f\\cdot g)(0)$, the product is not strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q8c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f\\circ g$ is strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.\n\nFor $a<b$, strict increase of $g$ gives $g(a)<g(b)$; strict increase of $f$ then gives $f(g(a))<f(g(b))$.",
          "worked": "Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ with $a<b$. Since $g$ is strictly increasing, $g(a)<g(b)$. Since $f$ is strictly increasing, $f(g(a))<f(g(b))$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(f\\circ g)(a)&=f(g(a))\\\\(f\\circ g)(a)&<f(g(b))\\\\(f\\circ g)(a)&<(f\\circ g)(b).\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence $f\\circ g$ is strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
